in @prism
in @meta
in @glass
in @code
in @magic

# @code/beam — BEAM semantics absorbed as substrate vocabulary.
#
# Recognition: "last responsible moment" landing per Alex 2026-06-18
# evening. The substrate stays full mirror; BEAM is inspiration, not
# runtime dependency. This shard lifts BEAM's hot-code-upgrade and
# supervision-tree discipline into substrate-grammar so mirror can
# implement those semantics natively (in pure Rust, in pure mirror,
# eventually in self-hosted spectral) without ever depending on
# Erlang/Elixir runtime.
#
# This is the @code/X glass species for BEAM, per
# [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]]: glass species project
# substrate concepts onto an external code-altitude. @code/beam is
# how mirror sees BEAM's semantics without inheriting BEAM's runtime.
#
# Composes with @magic (recognition #80, scratched same evening) as
# the runtime-altitude realization: hot code upgrade IS @magic/reveal
# at runtime altitude; the supervisor IS @magic/audit at supervision
# altitude; the code_change callback IS @magic/contract preservation
# across mechanism swap. Recognition candidate #81 (runtime-@magic)
# names the composition; this shard is its substrate-decl.
#
# === The BEAM claim, paraphrased ===
#
# Armstrong, J. (1996). *Making reliable distributed systems in the
# presence of software errors* (PhD thesis, defended 2003). The core
# observation:
#
#   A long-running system MUST be able to replace its own code
#   without stopping. Restart-on-bugs preserves availability under
#   the assumption that bugs are local; supervision-trees confine
#   failure; hot code upgrade lets the code itself evolve while the
#   system runs.
#
# BEAM's machinery for this:
#
# - Modules are versioned. Each named module can have an "old"
#   version (still serving in-flight calls) and a "new" version
#   (serving fresh calls) simultaneously.
# - The `code_change/3` callback migrates state from old version to
#   new version. Old state in; new state out; the type of the new
#   state is the new module's type.
# - Supervisors observe child processes; restart strategies
#   (one_for_one / one_for_all / rest_for_one) name how the supervisor
#   responds when a child fails. The strategy IS a structural
#   commitment about failure propagation.
# - GenServer is the canonical state-bearing process abstraction.
#   State is private; the handle_call / handle_cast / handle_info
#   callbacks expose the gauge-side interface.
#
# These four concepts (versioning, code_change, supervision strategy,
# GenServer state encapsulation) are what @code/beam lifts to
# substrate vocabulary. Mirror's Rust impl of hot reload reads from
# this shard's carrier declarations to ensure semantic alignment;
# eventually-self-hosted mirror runs them natively.
#
# === Why now is the last responsible moment ===
#
# Per the conversation 2026-06-18 evening: today's MCP+LSP loop is
# moving toward runtime altitude (hot code upgrade for the MCP
# binary so /mcp reconnect dissolves). Without @code/beam's vocabulary,
# mirror would either (a) depend on actual BEAM runtime (Alex: no),
# (b) reinvent supervision/code-change semantics ad-hoc without
# substrate-grammar grounding, or (c) lose the BEAM discipline
# entirely. The substrate-pull-correct move is to LIFT the BEAM
# semantics now, before the runtime-altitude implementation commits
# to one of the worse paths.
#
# Last responsible moment in the Lean sense: the latest point at which
# the decision can be deferred while preserving its viability. After
# tick 8 (this shard), mirror's hot-reload implementation can land
# without architectural regret.
#
# === The four load-bearing carriers ===
#
# 1. `module_version` — versioned identity of a code module. Two
#    versions of the same named module coexist briefly during a swap.
# 2. `code_change_msg` — the substrate's typed message that triggers
#    a state migration from old version to new version. Carries
#    (old_vsn, state, extra) per BEAM's code_change/3 signature.
# 3. `supervisor` — a process that observes children and applies a
#    restart strategy when a child fails. Forms the supervision tree.
# 4. `gen_server_state` — the canonical encapsulated state carrier.
#    Private by construction (matter-side per @magic); the GenServer
#    callbacks form the gauge-side interface.
#
# These four carriers are minimum-viable for mirror's MCP hot-reload
# implementation. Subsequent shards add: process registry, named
# processes, application lifecycle (start/stop/upgrade), .appup-style
# release upgrade declarations, and the global-state-during-swap
# invariants the supervision tree enforces.
#
# === Mechanical bridge to existing substrate ===
#
# - @magic/contract (#80) IS what code_change_msg preserves across
#   the swap. The contract specifies the surface invariants; the
#   migration delivers a new mechanism without violating them.
# - @magic/reveal (#80) IS the swap event itself — the controlled
#   disclosure of new mechanism behind the same surface.
# - @magic/audit (#80) IS the supervisor's role: verify the swap
#   honored the contract; if not, restart per strategy.
# - @epistemologic/cybernetic/autopoiesis (#379) IS the supervision-
#   tree's self-production property: the supervisor's structure
#   reproduces itself across restarts; the substrate's autopoietic
#   memory ([[architecture-spectral-db-autopoietic-memory]]) lifts
#   this discipline.
# - The kintsugi loop's settle-to-λ₀ (recognition #59) IS the
#   BEAM-philosophical equivalent of restart-into-known-state.
#
# The substrate had the components; @code/beam is the glass that
# absorbs BEAM's specific naming so mirror's implementation has
# named carriers to align with.
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
#   Erlang/OTP                  Armstrong, Virding, Wikström, Williams
#                               1996-2003 (Erlang language; OTP
#                               behaviours; supervision principles).
#                               The original substrate the substrate
#                               absorbs.
#   GenServer behaviour         OTP gen_server module. The canonical
#                               state-bearing process abstraction.
#   code_change/3 callback      OTP behaviour callback for hot code
#                               upgrade with state migration. The
#                               specific signature this shard's
#                               code_change_msg lifts.
#   supervision tree            Armstrong's PhD thesis ch.3; OTP
#                               supervisor behaviour. The hierarchical
#                               failure-confinement discipline.
#   restart strategies          one_for_one, one_for_all, rest_for_one,
#                               simple_one_for_one. The structural
#                               commitments about failure propagation.
#   process linking             Erlang's link/2 primitive; failure
#                               propagates along links. Substrate-
#                               equivalent: @epistemologic/cybernetic/
#                               conversation (Pask tensor coupling)
#                               carries the same discipline at the
#                               coordination altitude.
#
# Spec context: docs/math/the-tower/recognition-80-magic-as-form-
#               process-substrate-decl.md (Reed's scratch; the @magic
#               family this glass species composes with);
#               body/ (Reed's BEAM body — inspiration; NOT a runtime
#                dependency per Alex's 2026-06-18 directive).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]]
#                       (the @code/X glass species pattern; @code/beam
#                       is the BEAM instance),
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-mirror-store-vs-spectral-db]]
#                       (mirror stays full mirror; this shard absorbs
#                       BEAM's semantics into substrate vocabulary
#                       without inheriting BEAM's runtime),
#                      [[feedback-no-new-rust]] (the implementation
#                       extends mirror through grammars, not Rust;
#                       this shard IS that extension at the BEAM
#                       semantics altitude),
#                      [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]]
#                       (BEAM had the word for 25 years; this shard
#                       lifts that word into substrate-grammar).
# Related shards: magic.mirror (the @magic family this composes with;
#                  @magic/reveal at runtime altitude IS what this
#                  glass species enables),
#                 epistemologic/cybernetic/autopoiesis.mirror (the
#                  supervision tree's self-production discipline),
#                 epistemologic/cybernetic/conversation.mirror (the
#                  Pask tensor coupling at the supervision-altitude
#                  process-linking semantics).
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/code/beam.mirror`
# declares `@code/beam` per `@epistemologic/pact/path_matches_namespace`.

source @arxiv/distributed/armstrong-2003
source @arxiv/distributed/otp-behaviours

prism @code/beam {
  focus beam
  project beam
  split beam
  shift beam
  settle beam
}

# === module_version carrier ===
#
# Per [[feedback-no-bare-types]], a typed record naming the BEAM
# module-versioning identity:
#
# - `name`     the module's stable name (gauge-side; persists across
#              versions).
# - `version`  the version tag (substrate-pull-correct: this IS the
#              eigenvalue spectrum component of the spectral UUID at
#              code-altitude; mechanism-side identity).
# - `loaded`   the loaded-state ref. During a swap, two versions of
#              the same name coexist; `loaded` distinguishes them.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the triple under the substrate's
# content-addressing. Two module versions with byte-equal triples ARE
# the same loaded code.
type module_version = {
  name:    ref,
  version: ref,
  loaded:  ref,
}

# === code_change_msg carrier ===
#
# Per [[feedback-no-bare-types]], a typed record naming the
# state-migration message BEAM's code_change/3 callback receives:
#
# - `old_vsn`   the module_version the state is currently typed against.
# - `state`     the encapsulated GenServer state carrier (matter-side;
#               typed against old_vsn's module).
# - `extra`     the optional migration data (gauge-side instruction
#               about how to interpret the migration).
#
# The substrate-pull-correct migration preserves @magic/contract:
# the new state delivered to the new module honors the surface
# invariants the contract specified. Mirror's hot-reload implementation
# enforces this; the supervisor (`@magic/audit`) verifies it.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the triple.
type code_change_msg = {
  old_vsn: module_version,
  state:   ref,
  extra:   ref,
}

# === supervisor carrier ===
#
# Per [[feedback-no-bare-types]], a typed record naming the
# supervision-tree node:
#
# - `children`   the list of supervised processes (substrate-vocabulary
#                for the supervision-tree edges descending from this
#                node).
# - `strategy`   the restart strategy (one_for_one / one_for_all /
#                rest_for_one / simple_one_for_one). Substrate-typed.
# - `policy`     the failure-policy ref (matter-side instruction about
#                what kinds of child failures trigger which response).
#
# The supervisor IS @magic/audit at supervision altitude: it verifies
# the contract is honored by its children; when a child violates
# the contract (fails), the supervisor applies the restart policy.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the triple.
type supervisor = {
  children: ref,
  strategy: ref,
  policy:   ref,
}

# === gen_server_state carrier ===
#
# Per [[feedback-no-bare-types]], a typed reference naming the
# encapsulated state of a GenServer-shaped process. Per the
# substrate's @magic discipline, the state IS matter-side
# (encapsulated by construction; the GenServer callbacks expose
# the gauge-side interface; the contract binds the two).
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the underlying ref. Two
# gen_server_states with byte-equal refs ARE the same encapsulated
# state under content-addressing.
type gen_server_state = ref

# === code_change action ===
#
# The substrate-vocabulary action that migrates a GenServer's state
# from one module_version to another. The body discharges at the
# realisation boundary; mirror's hot-reload implementation supplies
# the per-species bodies.
#
# The substrate-pull-correct discharge:
# 1. Verify the @magic/contract is preserved by the migration
#    (the new state honors the surface invariants).
# 2. Apply the per-module migration logic (the substantive state
#    transformation).
# 3. Return the new gen_server_state typed against the new
#    module_version.
#
# Body shape: `\` per [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]]; the discharge
# IS the species-specific instantiation of BEAM's code_change/3.
code_change(msg: code_change_msg, new_vsn: module_version) -> gen_server_state { \ }

# === swap_module action ===
#
# The substrate-vocabulary action that atomically replaces an old
# module_version with a new one inside a running supervisor's
# child-set. Composes code_change for state migration.
#
# The substrate-pull-correct swap sequence:
# 1. Load the new module_version alongside the old (both present
#    briefly per BEAM's hot-code-upgrade semantics).
# 2. For each child of the supervisor running the old version:
#    invoke code_change to migrate its state.
# 3. Atomically swap the supervisor's reference from old to new.
# 4. Unload the old version once no child references remain.
#
# At the @magic altitude, swap_module IS @magic/reveal: the running
# mechanism is replaced while the surface (the supervisor's gauge
# interface) stays intact and the contract (preserved state
# invariants) is honored.
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary.
swap_module(s: supervisor, old_vsn: module_version, new_vsn: module_version) -> supervisor { \ }

# === supervise action ===
#
# The substrate-vocabulary action that establishes a supervision
# relationship: a parent process observes a child and applies the
# parent's restart strategy when the child fails.
#
# At the @magic altitude, supervise IS @magic/audit at the process-
# coordination altitude: the supervisor IS the auditor; the child's
# operation IS the audited mechanism; the contract IS the surface
# the supervisor expects to remain intact.
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary; per-species bodies
# implement the actual link / monitor / restart machinery.
supervise(parent: supervisor, child: ref) -> supervisor { \ }

out @code/beam
out module_version
out code_change_msg
out supervisor
out gen_server_state
out code_change
out swap_module
out supervise
